// @emit-error literal-union string widening with no members
// CONTRACT (must-emit-reject) — the pin `std/array.vl`'s surviving carve-out did not have,
// and the reason it did not have one is the point. That carve-out documents an honest emit
// refusal on this module's generic surface, and NOTHING IN THE TREE would have said when it
// moved: the message is pinned for other shapes, never for a `std:array` type parameter. Its
// neighbour `error-array-struct-element.vl` is the exact in-directory precedent — an honest
// refusal on the same surface, given its own `error-*` file so it cannot go stale silently.
//
// The carve-out this replaces was noticed only because the same branch happened to fix the
// defect beside it. That is luck, not a process, and the module header's own closing rule
// (re-probe the section when the family under it moves, INCLUDING toward correctness) has
// no mechanism behind it without files like this one.
//
// THE FILE HAS ALREADY EARNED ITS KEEP, and this header is the record of that. It was
// written for TWO spellings — `mapIndexed`'s result `U` and `reduce`'s accumulator `A` — and
// `mapIndexed`'s HALF GRADUATED when the monomorphizer stopped spelling one type parameter
// two ways inside one instance. Because emission stops at the first refusal and both
// reported the identical message, nothing but this file's continued red would have said
// which half was still live. That half is now a `@run` row in
// `tests/cases/std/array-mapindexed-litunion-result.vl`, and only `reduce` is left below.
//
// THE SHAPE, re-probed rather than inherited: `reduce`'s accumulator `A` bound to a string
// literal union. `K` reps as the interned i32 ATOM and the widen to member text has no member
// list to work from at this boundary, so emit refuses rather than building a value it cannot
// spell.
//
// IT IS NOT ABOUT THE ALIAS, which is what this header said before the axis was probed, and
// it is not a `reduce` bug. A hand-written generic importing nothing reproduces it, and the
// deciding property is PARAMETER ORDER: `A` is first bound at `f: (A, T) => A`, a CALLBACK
// parameter. Move `init: A` ahead of `f` and the IDENTICAL body runs — measured, on the same
// program, which is the A/B this row is scoped on. `reduce(self, f, init)` cannot take that
// remedy; the argument order is the conventional one.
//
// AND THE INLINE SPELLING IS NOT A REMEDY. An inline `"a" | "b"` accumulator runs only while
// no same-layout alias is declared anywhere in the COMPILED PROGRAM — adding an unused
// `type Unrelated = "a" | "b"` is enough to bring the refusal back, and `std:fs`'s exported
// `FileKind = "file" | "dir"` would do it to a `"file" | "dir"` accumulator by import alone.
// The remedy that holds, measured in a module that DOES declare the alias: type the
// accumulator `string` and narrow at the use site.
import { reduce } from "std:array"

type K = "a" | "b"

function pick(a: K, x: i32): K {
  if x > 0 { return "b" }
  return a
}

const init: K = "a"
print([0, 1].reduce(pick, init))
